  1. Getters return Encode's canonical name (utf-8-strict, iso-8859-1), and locale is resolved when it's set. This is the
     contradiction you asked about. If $cs->argv returned the string 'locale', then $cs->stdout($cs->argv) would turn it into
     stdout's own locale (console_out), not argv's encoding. Unset is still undef, so your -l idiom works. Error messages use the
     MIME name (in US-ASCII, in UTF-8).
  2. I didn't use Dumper Useqq. Both diag modes share one escaper: \t \n \r \f \b \a \e by name, \x{..} for everything else, and "
     \ $ @ always escaped. Dumper writes \211 or \x{89} for the same character depending on how the string happens to be stored.
     Mine always writes \x{89}. I can switch back if you'd rather.
  3. new and parse apply settings in order, so console overrides what came before it and a later stderr:... overrides console.
     Getting console, console_in or console_out returns their encoding only when all their categories agree, otherwise undef.
  4. -C diag on its own means unicode. "Omitted means UTF-8" doesn't fit a mode. diag_mode is checked against stderr each time it's
     read, unless it was set explicitly.
  5. un_argv/to_argv give errors like cannot decode argv 2: ascii "\xE9" does not map to Unicode, counting from 1 (that's my
     choice). On failure the array is left exactly as it was.
  6. setenv changes all the variables or none, and undef unsets one, as in IPC::Unix. Variable names are encoded as well as values.
  7. to_fs/un_fs keep the optional $who, so GenGenLib can hand off to them unchanged. The message used to say in current locale (X)
     and now says in <enc>. gengenlib's t/unicode.t regex still matches, but I'll grep for current locale when moving things over.
  8. Errors raised by the library always use ASCII escaping, whatever diag_mode is set to, as the old _perlqq did. initstd uses
     :raw:encoding(...), so calling it twice doesn't encode twice.
  9. POD headings use B<name>([ ... ]) rather than [( ... )], because Pod::Coverage can't parse the latter.

