  quick question though: the lower ASCIIs use octal or un-demarcated hex, right?

⏺ Neither. Every character that has no named escape comes out as braced hex, sprintf '\x{%x}', ord $c, whatever its code point:

  - "\x1a" → \x{1a}. Dumper gives \32 for this.
  - "\0" → \x{0}
  - "\x7f" → \x{7f}
  - "\x89" → \x{89}. Dumper gives \211, or \x{89} if the string happens to be upgraded.

  The named ones stay named: \t \n \r \f \b \a \e.

  Braces avoid the usual problems with the other two forms. Octal needs care when a digit follows: Dumper writes \0 followed by 1
  as \0001 to keep it unambiguous. Bare \xHH stops after two digits, so it's safe in Perl, but it looks the same as a longer escape
  when followed by hex-like text, as in \x1aB. With braces, a control character and a CJK character are written the same way, as
  in \x{1a} and \x{7092}. The test in t/diag.t checks this: "\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n" becomes "\x{89}PNG\r\n\x{1a}\n" in both modes.

